\description{
Tests whether groups of samples differ in how spread out they are: their
multivariate dispersion, or beta diversity. Gives the same results as
\code{vegan::betadisper()} followed by \code{vegan::permutest()}: each sample's
distance to its group's centre, each group's mean distance, and a
permutation test of whether those distances differ between groups.
}
\details{
A significant PERMANOVA (\code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}}) can come from groups that differ
in location, in dispersion, or both. A non-significant \code{permdisp()} result
supports reading a PERMANOVA difference as a difference in location.

\strong{Method}

PERMDISP (Anderson 2006) places the samples in the principal coordinates
of \code{dm} (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}), finds each group's centre, and measures each sample's
distance to it. For distances that are not Euclidean, such as
Bray-Curtis, some principal coordinates are imaginary (their eigenvalues
are negative); following Anderson (2006), a sample's squared distance to
its centre is the squared distance on the real axes minus that on the
imaginary ones, and is set to zero, with a warning, when that is negative.

The test is the one-way ANOVA \eqn{F} of these distances. As in
\code{permutest()}, the p-value comes from permuting the ANOVA's residuals (each
distance minus its group's mean) among the samples: the proportion of
permutations, counting the observed one, with an \eqn{F} at least as
large, \eqn{(1 + \#\{F_{perm} \ge F\}) / (1 + permutations)}. Permuted
statistics within \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} of the observed one count as
ties. Pairwise \eqn{t} statistics permute the distances themselves, and
count \eqn{|t_{perm}| \ge |t|}.

\strong{Choosing \code{type}}
\itemize{
\item \code{'median'}, the default, is what \code{vegan::betadisper()} and scikit-bio's
(and so QIIME 2's) \code{permdisp} use by default. Like the median-based
(Brown-Forsythe) version of Levene's test, it is less affected by
skewed distributions and outlying samples (Anderson 2006).
\item \code{'centroid'} measures dispersion around the same centres that
\code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}} does: the squared distances to the centroids sum to
PERMANOVA's within-group sum of squares. So it is the direct check of
whether a significant PERMANOVA could come from differences in
dispersion (Anderson 2006; Anderson et al. 2006). It is also exact and
much faster for many samples (see below).
}

\code{betadisper()} stops its search for the spatial median early, so its
distances to medians are accurate only to about \eqn{10^{-5}};
\code{permdisp()} solves for the median exactly.

\strong{Speed and memory}

A sample's distance to its group's centroid needs only the distances
within its group: \deqn{z_i^2 = \frac{1}{n_g}\sum_{j \in g} d_{ij}^2 -
\frac{1}{2n_g^2}\sum_{j,k \in g} d_{jk}^2,} which already accounts for
the imaginary axes. So \code{type = 'centroid'} needs no principal coordinates
at all. The spatial median does need them: a full eigendecomposition of
an \eqn{n \times n} matrix, which takes about as long as in
\code{betadisper()} (a few seconds for 1000 samples). Each permutation then
costs only one pass over the \eqn{n} distances, and permutations run in
parallel.

\strong{Reproducibility}

The permutations come from \code{seed}, not from R's random number generator,
so \code{set.seed()} has no effect, and each permutation has a random number
stream of its own. Results are identical from run to run and for any
number of \code{cpus}. The random permutations differ from \code{permutest()}'s, so
p-values agree with it only up to sampling error, unless the same
\code{permutations} matrix is given to both.

\strong{Differences from betadisper() and permutest()}
\itemize{
\item \code{type = 'centroid'} gives the same distances and \eqn{F} as
\code{betadisper()}, to rounding error.
\item \code{type = 'median'} finds each spatial median to full precision.
\code{betadisper()} stops its optimizer (\code{optim()}'s BFGS) early, so its
distances to the median are off by up to about \eqn{10^{-5}} (relative),
and its \eqn{F} agrees with \code{permdisp()}'s to about 4 or 5 significant
digits.
\item Samples with an \code{NA} group are an error, where \code{betadisper()} drops
them.
\item With \code{bias_adjust = TRUE}, a group of one sample keeps a distance of 0,
where \code{betadisper()} gives \code{NaN}.
\item Pairwise permutation p-values count permuted \eqn{|t|} within
\code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} of the observed one as ties, as for
\eqn{F}.
\item \code{strata} permutes freely within strata. For other restricted designs,
pass a \code{permutations} matrix from \code{permute::shuffleSet()}.
\item \code{betadisper()}'s \code{sqrt.dist} and \code{add} options transform the distances
first: pass \code{sqrt(dm)} instead, for instance.
}
}
\examples{
    # 30 samples in three groups. The 'high' group is more variable.
    set.seed(1)
    counts <- matrix(rpois(30 * 20, 5), 30, 20)
    rownames(counts) <- paste0('S', 1:30)
    meta <- data.frame(
      row.names = rownames(counts),
      treatment = rep(c('ctrl', 'low', 'high'), each = 10) )
    high <- meta$treatment == 'high'
    counts[high, ] <- counts[high, ] * rpois(10 * 20, 1)

    dm <- bray(counts)

    # Do the treatment groups differ in dispersion?
    res <- permdisp(dm, 'treatment', meta)
    res

    # Each group's mean distance to its spatial median.
    res$dispersions

    # Distances to centroids, with each pair of groups tested.
    res <- permdisp(dm, meta$treatment, type = 'centroid', pairwise = TRUE)
    res$pairwise

}
